<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n134" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Abnŏba</span></b> , ae, m., <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>a mountain range in
				Germany</i>, <i>the northern part of the Black
				Forest</i>, <i>in which the Danube rises</i>,
				<i><span class="en">Plin.
				4, 12, 24, § 79</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Tac. G.
			1</span></i>; cf. Mannert, Germ. p. 512.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b> Hence, <b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Abnŏba Diana</span></b> , or simply <b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Abnŏba</span></b> , ae,
			f., <i>the goddess of this mountain</i>,
					<i><span class="en">Inscr. Orell. 1986</span></i> and
		4974.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
